.TH GDB-QUICKREF 7 "2026-04-27" "CECS 378" "CECS 378 Buffer Overflow Lab"
.SH NAME
gdb-quickref \- gdb commands you will need for the buffer-overflow lab
.SH DESCRIPTION
A reference for the gdb subset relevant to this lab. Not a tutorial.
For more, read
.BR gdb (1)
or the GNU
.B gdb
manual on the box (\fBinfo gdb\fR).

.SS STARTING

.TP
.B "gdb /opt/lab/build/vuln1"
Open vuln1 with full debug symbols.
.TP
.B "    (gdb) run AAAAAAAA"
Run with an argument.
.TP
.B "    (gdb) run $(python3 -c 'print(\"A\"*200)')"
Run with a pattern long enough to overflow.

.SS BREAKPOINTS

.TP
.B "    (gdb) disas exploitable"
Disassemble the function. Find the address right after the
.BR strcpy (3)
call.
.TP
.B "    (gdb) break *0x080491a4"
Break at that address.
.TP
.B "    (gdb) break exploitable"
Break at function entry.

.SS INSPECTION

.TP
.B "    (gdb) info frame"
Print the current stack frame: addresses of saved EIP, saved EBP,
arguments, locals.
.TP
.B "    (gdb) x/40wx $esp"
Dump 40 32-bit words from the stack pointer.
.TP
.B "    (gdb) x/s $eax"
Dump the string at EAX (after a strcpy, EAX often holds the
destination).
.TP
.B "    (gdb) info functions"
List all known functions.
.TP
.B "    (gdb) info shared"
List loaded shared libraries (libc lives here).
.TP
.B "    (gdb) print system"
Print the address of
.BR system (3).
.TP
.B "    (gdb) print exit"
Print the address of
.BR exit (3).

.SS FINDING /bin/sh IN libc

Outside gdb:
.IP
.B "strings -a -t x /lib/i386-linux-gnu/libc.so.6 | grep '/bin/sh'"
.PP
Add the offset (left column, hex) to the libc base address (\fIinfo
shared\fR in gdb) to get the runtime address.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mainframe (7),
.BR phase-phi (7),
.BR phase-omega (7),
.BR gdb (1)

.SH BUGS
gdb is not the bug. The vulnerable program is the bug.
